            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Allrecipes Member"
            },
            "reviewBody": "For the dry/bland comments ... are y'all using RAW bulk breakfast sausage? The sausage should NOT be cooked before mixing into the other ingredients. The oil that cooks out of the sausage should keep the bread part of the mixture soft and moist and make it flavorful. You can always use 'hot' or 'sage' sausage to add more spice if the 'mild' is too blah ... or add a dash of cayenne pepper to the mix. Cooking time may need to be reduced ... just watch for golden brown cheese balls."
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Vicki Lubarsky"
            },
            "reviewBody": "First, sausage should be sage or hot jimmy dean bulk sausage at close to room temp, 2c bisquick and 3 C sharp cheddar at room temp. The room temp makes it easier to combine. 1/2 c milk can be added, though I never do. Roll into small balls and bake. They are rich but heavenly if made correctly!",
            "datePublished": "2017-12-27T02:31:14.523Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "3"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "RogerB"
            },
            "reviewBody": "made them used 1lb sausage 1lb sharp cheddar cheese and two cups baking mix and then used a mixer with a dough hook cut prep time way down",
            "datePublished": "2016-12-22T05:31:29.710Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "sanditeaches"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I've made this every Christmas for the last 50 years and my famiiy always loves them. I add green chiles and a small jar of pimentos using the juice (no milk) to get a little red and green color for the holidays as well as extra flavor! Try it--",
            "datePublished": "2022-11-07T15:56:31.043Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "PaintedLady"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I've been making these for years. My family loves them I think the secret to making them more tasty is to use sharp cheddar cheese and half of the sausage, use spicy/hot or sage flavored. I don't use as much of the mix as is called for. For 2 lbs of sausage &amp; 1 lb of cheese, I use 3 cups of mix (bis quick). No more. Spray cookie sheet with non stick spray. Bake at 350 for 10 to 15 mins (depending on oven). Serve warm, but don't try to keep them hot/warm, it only dries them out. Enjoy! Thanks for the tip for the veggie ones to use olive oil. I make those for my daughter.",
            "datePublished": "2013-12-22T09:06:02.817Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Lola_S"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I followed another reviewers, instructions and used gluten free biscuit mix from aldi, mixed with hot sausage and italian sausage (i also added additional sage), lots of shredded cheddar. Holy smokes, I did not think this would be as popular as it was, but it was THE dish at a work potluck that was gone first (and I made a LoT because its so I expensive to make), then I made it again for two holiday parties and everyone was asking me for the recipe. So simple, so inexpensive, and a proven hit! I'm keeping this one.",
            "datePublished": "2024-12-28T22:29:39.376Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Happyschmoopies"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I have a recipe that I make like this, but, using less bisquick. I thought I would try this one and see the difference in taste. I started out with 1-1/2 cups of bisquick and gradually increased. I ended up using only just a little over two cups of the mix and they turned out perfect. I also baked at 350 for the same time because I know my oven bakes a little hot. I left the sausage and cheese measurements the same. Very yummy!",
            "datePublished": "2012-08-04T08:51:48.813Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Amy McElroy"
            },
            "reviewBody": "These were really good. I did modify a little though instead of adding the batter to the sausage I actually cooked the sausage ( I mixed maple and sage sausage together) and placed in paper towels. I omitted the cheese do to food allergies. Let them cool completely (I put them in the fridge) and made pancake batter dipped the balls in there and baked until the batter was golden brown. Served with maple syrup and everyone went crazy for them on christmas morning.",
            "datePublished": "2007-12-26T11:28:29.143Z"
          },
          {
